 "crate": "chrono",
 "version": "0.4.45",
 "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
 "kind": "ported",
 "sys": null,
 "quality_assessment": "Not fit: coverage. It has no mktime: no normalisation of out-of-range fields and no tm_isdst hint, so the clocks and calendar time area it was assessed for stays with the crustify campaign. Coverage: Calendar conversion only: Utc/Local from a timestamp (gmtime_r, localtime_r), reading TZ and /etc/localtime with its own tzfile parser. It has no mktime equivalent: it does not normalise out-of-range tm fields (date.c:564 relies on that) and takes no tm_isdst hint (signature.c:172 relies on mktime reading a gmtime result with tm_isdst=0 as standard time). Local::from_local_datetime returns ambiguous or none instead. clock_gettime, gettimeofday, time and difftime are std's (Instant, SystemTime). Shared state: It keeps its state in Rust: nothing it holds crosses to C. Soundness (sound): 10 unsafe blocks, no public unsafe fn. On Linux they are a NonZeroI32::new_unchecked on a checked value (naive/date/mod.rs:1496), from_utf8_unchecked over validated tz names (offset/local/tz_info/timezone.rs:550) and CStr::from_bytes_with_nul_unchecked after locating the NUL (offset/local/tz_data.rs:158). Since 0.4.20 it no longer calls libc localtime_r (lib.rs:457-472). No RustSec advisory affects 0.4.45. Maintenance: last released 2026-06-04, published by the chronotope organization (github:chronotope:release-manager) and lifthrasiir; license MIT OR Apache-2.0."
}
{
 "crate": "nix",
 "version": "0.31.3",
 "license": "MIT",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "libc",
 "quality_assessment": "Fit for files, directories and file metadata; sockets, name resolution and polling; process identity, users and system information; and errno and error strings. Coverage: Calls glibc itself, so behaviour and errno side effects are glibc's. Covers files: open/fcntl (O_FSYNC on Linux gnu), stat/lstat/fstat with FileStat = libc::stat, fchmodat, mkdir, utimes, futimens, readlink, access, linkat/symlinkat/renameat/unlinkat (for rmdir), fsync, lseek, read/write, pread/pwrite, getcwd and Dir. Covers sockets: socket, connect, send, recv, sockopt KeepAlive/TcpKeepIdle/TcpKeepInterval/TcpKeepCount/SocketError, and poll. Also covers the get*id calls, sysconf, User::from_uid (getpwuid_r), sysinfo load_average, Errno last/set, pthread_sigmask, SigSet and SigSet::wait (sigwait), execve, pipe, dup2, chdir and waitpid. Lacks realpath, getaddrinfo/gai_strerror and inet_pton (std covers these), sigpending and getentropy. fork, signal, sigaction, mmap and munmap are unsafe fns. Errno::desc is nix's own table, so strerror text differs (EAGAIN gives 'Try again'; glibc gives 'Resource temporarily unavailable'). getloadavg goes through sysinfo(2) loads at 1/65536 precision. No stdio, string, ctype, qsort, calendar conversion, pthread mutex/rwlock/cond/key or malloc. Shared state: It exposes the state it holds to C (raw descriptors or handles). Soundness (sound): 749 unsafe blocks and 59 pub unsafe fns; the operations unsafe by nature are unsafe fns (unistd.rs:278 fork, sys/signal.rs:930 sigaction, :992 signal, sys/mman.rs:427 mmap, :540 munmap). fds are std OwnedFd/BorrowedFd/AsFd, and PollFd<'fd> carries the borrow (poll.rs:18-20). Dir owns a NonNull<DIR> and is Send but not Sync (dir.rs:47,124). Growing buffers pass their capacity and set_len to the returned length (unistd.rs:937-950 getcwd, fcntl.rs:554-631 readlink, unistd.rs:3714-3745 getpwuid_r). Not UB: from_anything tests Errno::last()==ERANGE rather than getpwuid_r's return value (unistd.rs:3740). No RustSec advisory affects 0.31.3. Maintenance: last released 2026-05-11, published by the nix-rust organization's nix-maintainers team and one maintainer (carllerche); license MIT."
}
{
 "crate": "rustix",
 "version": "1.1.6",
 "license": "Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "linux-raw-sys",
 "quality_assessment": "Not fit: maintenance. One crates.io owner publishes it; it otherwise covers files and sockets. Coverage: Covers files: open, stat/lstat/fstat, chmod, mkdir, rmdir, unlink, link, symlink, readlink, rename, access, utimensat/futimens, fsync, seek, read/write/pread/pwrite, fcntl_getfl/setfl/getfd/setfd, getcwd, chdir and Dir. Covers sockets: socket_with, connect, send, recv, keepalive/keepidle/keepintvl/keepcnt, socket_error and poll. Also covers the get*id calls, page_size, getrandom, clock_gettime, sysinfo loads, pipe, dup2 and waitpid. Lacks realpath, getaddrinfo, inet_pton, getpwuid_r, sysconf (_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN, _SC_GETPW_R_SIZE_MAX) and an errno setter. fork, execve and signal masks exist only in its unsafe, semver-exempt runtime feature; mmap and munmap are unsafe fns. Its default Linux backend makes raw syscalls and leaves errno untouched, so a ported function whose C callers read errno must set errno some other way (the libc backend, --cfg rustix_use_libc, leaves glibc's errno). It fails the maintenance criterion alone: one crates.io owner publishes it. Shared state: It exposes the state it holds to C (raw descriptors or handles). Soundness (sound): 1067 unsafe blocks and 70 pub unsafe fns, mostly mm/mmap.rs (9), the runtime module (16) and io_uring; none in the file, socket or id calls used. Every call takes AsFd/BorrowedFd, output buffers go through the sealed Buffer trait (buffer.rs:106-165), and Dir owns an OwnedFd (backend/linux_raw/fs/dir.rs:17-19). The xattr zero-length-buffer UB (RUSTSEC-2026-0486) is fixed in 1.1.6 and outside the used surface. No RustSec advisory affects 1.1.6. Maintenance: last released 2026-10-27, published by one crates.io owner (sunfishcode), from the bytecodealliance organization's repository; license Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT."
}
{
 "crate": "socket2",
 "version": "0.6.5",
 "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "libc",
 "quality_assessment": "Fit for sockets, name resolution and polling; nix is recommended there, as it covers the other areas too. Coverage: Sockets only. Socket::new sets SOCK_CLOEXEC (socket.rs:744-), connect_timeout does a non-blocking connect, poll and SO_ERROR as socket.c does (socket.rs:215, sys/unix.rs:973), and it has set_tcp_keepalive(TcpKeepalive with_time/with_interval/with_retries), take_error, send/recv and set_nonblocking. Lacks getaddrinfo/gai_strerror and inet_pton (std::net covers them) and a general poll: its send/recv timeouts are SO_SNDTIMEO/SO_RCVTIMEO, where socket.c polls on EAGAIN. Shared state: It exposes the state it holds to C (raw descriptors or handles). Soundness (sound): 215 unsafe blocks and 3 pub unsafe fns (sockaddr.rs:73 view_as, :197 try_init, :232 set_length). Socket wraps an OwnedFd (sys/unix.rs:940); raw fds enter only through unsafe FromRawFd (sys/unix.rs:3057) or the crate-private from_raw fed by syscalls (socket.rs:86). The sockaddr layout cast of RUSTSEC-2020-0079 is fixed since 0.3.16. No RustSec advisory affects 0.6.5. Maintenance: last released 2026-07-13, published by the rust-lang organization (repo rust-lang/socket2; owners sfackler, Thomasdezeeuw, rust-lang-owner, github:tokio-rs:socket2); license MIT OR Apache-2.0."
}
{
 "crate": "memmap2",
 "version": "0.9.12",
 "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "libc",
 "quality_assessment": "Not fit: coverage and shared state. Its file-backed constructors are unsafe fns and it cannot adopt a mapping C created. Coverage: Memory maps only: Mmap and MmapMut over a file (MAP_SHARED with write through map_mut, MAP_PRIVATE through map_copy). Every file-backed constructor is an unsafe fn (lib.rs:557, 633, 677, 933, 1453). The only safe ones are map_if_safe, for sealed memfds or fs-verity files only (lib.rs:757), and MmapRaw, which yields a raw pointer. It cannot adopt or release a mapping given as (data, len), so a git_map created or unmapped by C cannot be shared. Shared state: It keeps its state in Rust: nothing it holds crosses to C. Soundness (sound): 72 unsafe blocks and 17 pub unsafe fns. Its safe Deref gives a slice over the mapping it owns (lib.rs:1103, 1698, 1705), with Send/Sync on MmapInner (unix.rs:658-659). The constructors' contract, that the file is not modified or truncated, is left to the caller. The out-of-range advise/flush offsets of RUSTSEC-2026-0186 are fixed in 0.9.11. No RustSec advisory affects 0.9.12. Maintenance: last released 2026-10-21, published by four maintainers (tbu-, RazrFalcon, de-vri-es, allan2); license MIT OR Apache-2.0."
}
