{
 "crate": "chrono",
 "version": "0.4.45",
 "license": "MIT OR Apache-2.0",
 "kind": "ported",
 "sys": null,
 "quality_assessment": "Not fit: coverage. It has no mktime: no normalisation of out-of-range fields and no tm_isdst hint, so the clocks and calendar time area it was assessed for stays with the crustify campaign. Coverage: Calendar conversion only: Utc/Local from a timestamp (gmtime_r, localtime_r), reading TZ and /etc/localtime with its own tzfile parser. It has no mktime equivalent: it does not normalise out-of-range tm fields (date.c:564 relies on that) and takes no tm_isdst hint (signature.c:172 relies on mktime reading a gmtime result with tm_isdst=0 as standard time). Local::from_local_datetime returns ambiguous or none instead. clock_gettime, gettimeofday, time and difftime are std's (Instant, SystemTime). Shared state: It keeps its state in Rust: nothing it holds crosses to C. Soundness (sound): 10 unsafe blocks, no public unsafe fn. On Linux they are a NonZeroI32::new_unchecked on a checked value (naive/date/mod.rs:1496), from_utf8_unchecked over validated tz names (offset/local/tz_info/timezone.rs:550) and CStr::from_bytes_with_nul_unchecked after locating the NUL (offset/local/tz_data.rs:158). Since 0.4.20 it no longer calls libc localtime_r (lib.rs:457-472). No RustSec advisory affects 0.4.45. Maintenance: last released 2026-06-04, published by the chronotope organization (github:chronotope:release-manager) and lifthrasiir; license MIT OR Apache-2.0."
}
{
 "crate": "nix",
 "version": "0.31.3",
 "license": "MIT",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "libc",
 "quality_assessment": "Fit for files, directories and file metadata; sockets, name resolution and polling; process identity, users and system information; and errno and error strings. Coverage: Calls glibc itself, so behaviour and errno side effects are glibc's. Covers files: open/fcntl (O_FSYNC on Linux gnu), stat/lstat/fstat with FileStat = libc::stat, fchmodat, mkdir, utimes, futimens, readlink, access, linkat/symlinkat/renameat/unlinkat (for rmdir), fsync, lseek, read/write, pread/pwrite, getcwd and Dir. Covers sockets: socket, connect, send, recv, sockopt KeepAlive/TcpKeepIdle/TcpKeepInterval/TcpKeepCount/SocketError, and poll. Also covers the get*id calls, sysconf, User::from_uid (getpwuid_r), sysinfo load_average, Errno last/set, pthread_sigmask, SigSet and SigSet::wait (sigwait), execve, pipe, dup2, chdir and waitpid. Lacks realpath, getaddrinfo/gai_strerror and inet_pton (std covers these), sigpending and getentropy. fork, signal, sigaction, mmap and munmap are unsafe fns. Errno::desc is nix's own table, so strerror text differs (EAGAIN gives 'Try again'; glibc gives 'Resource temporarily unavailable'). getloadavg goes through sysinfo(2) loads at 1/65536 precision. No stdio, string, ctype, qsort, calendar conversion, pthread mutex/rwlock/cond/key or malloc. Shared state: It exposes the state it holds to C (raw descriptors or handles). Soundness (sound): 749 unsafe blocks and 59 pub unsafe fns; the operations unsafe by nature are unsafe fns (unistd.rs:278 fork, sys/signal.rs:930 sigaction, :992 signal, sys/mman.rs:427 mmap, :540 munmap). fds are std OwnedFd/BorrowedFd/AsFd, and PollFd<'fd> carries the borrow (poll.rs:18-20). Dir owns a NonNull<DIR> and is Send but not Sync (dir.rs:47,124). Growing buffers pass their capacity and set_len to the returned length (unistd.rs:937-950 getcwd, fcntl.rs:554-631 readlink, unistd.rs:3714-3745 getpwuid_r). Not UB: from_anything tests Errno::last()==ERANGE rather than getpwuid_r's return value (unistd.rs:3740). No RustSec advisory affects 0.31.3. Maintenance: last released 2026-05-11, published by the nix-rust organization's nix-maintainers team and one maintainer (carllerche); license MIT."
}
{
 "crate": "rustix",
