 "license": "Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT",
 "kind": "bindings",
 "sys": "linux-raw-sys",
 "quality_assessment": "Not fit: maintenance. One crates.io owner publishes it; it otherwise covers files and sockets. Coverage: Covers files: open, stat/lstat/fstat, chmod, mkdir, rmdir, unlink, link, symlink, readlink, rename, access, utimensat/futimens, fsync, seek, read/write/pread/pwrite, fcntl_getfl/setfl/getfd/setfd, getcwd, chdir and Dir. Covers sockets: socket_with, connect, send, recv, keepalive/keepidle/keepintvl/keepcnt, socket_error and poll. Also covers the get*id calls, page_size, getrandom, clock_gettime, sysinfo loads, pipe, dup2 and waitpid. Lacks realpath, getaddrinfo, inet_pton, getpwuid_r, sysconf (_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN, _SC_GETPW_R_SIZE_MAX) and an errno setter. fork, execve and signal masks exist only in its unsafe, semver-exempt runtime feature; mmap and munmap are unsafe fns. Its default Linux backend makes raw syscalls and leaves errno untouched, so a ported function whose C callers read errno must set errno some other way (the libc backend, --cfg rustix_use_libc, leaves glibc's errno). It fails the maintenance criterion alone: one crates.io owner publishes it. Shared state: It exposes the state it holds to C (raw descriptors or handles). Soundness (sound): 1067 unsafe blocks and 70 pub unsafe fns, mostly mm/mmap.rs (9), the runtime module (16) and io_uring; none in the file, socket or id calls used. Every call takes AsFd/BorrowedFd, output buffers go through the sealed Buffer trait (buffer.rs:106-165), and Dir owns an OwnedFd (backend/linux_raw/fs/dir.rs:17-19). The xattr zero-length-buffer UB (RUSTSEC-2026-0486) is fixed in 1.1.6 and outside the used surface. No RustSec advisory affects 1.1.6. Maintenance: last released 2026-10-27, published by one crates.io owner (sunfishcode), from the bytecodealliance organization's repository; license Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT."
